1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:05,784
A payment just ended: how does the shop's server find out, without asking again and again?

2
00:00:05,834 --> 00:00:14,520
If the payment were saved first and the news published after it, a crash in between would lose the news forever, or announce a payment that never committed.

3
00:00:14,570 --> 00:00:22,200
So the news goes into an outbox table, in the same transaction as the final status: they commit together, or not at all.

4
00:00:22,250 --> 00:00:29,376
The payment raises the event itself, whenever it becomes paid, declined, failed, or sent to manual review.

5
00:00:29,426 --> 00:00:34,920
A listener writes it to the outbox while the payment is saved, inside that same transaction.

6
00:00:34,970 --> 00:00:45,264
A relay runs every second, takes the oldest unpublished rows, and locks them with skip locked, so two instances never wait on each other or send the same row.

7
00:00:45,314 --> 00:00:53,976
It publishes each one to Kafka, keyed by the payment, so one payment's events stay in order, and marks it published only after Kafka has it.

8
00:00:54,026 --> 00:01:00,624
A crash in between sends it again, so every event carries its own id, and consumers drop repeats.

9
00:01:00,674 --> 00:01:07,128
The merchant sets one webhook address per website: HTTPS, on the website's own domain.

10
00:01:07,178 --> 00:01:18,648
A Kafka listener posts the event there, signed the Standard Webhooks way with the merchant's key: an event id, a timestamp, and an HMAC signature that any Standard Webhooks library can check.

11
00:01:18,698 --> 00:01:26,136
Anything but a two hundred is retried: one second, then two, then four, up to thirty, five times.

12
00:01:26,186 --> 00:01:30,432
After that, the event is parked on a dead-letter topic for a person.

13
00:01:30,482 --> 00:01:34,584
Every attempt is logged: where it went, and what the merchant answered.

14
00:01:34,634 --> 00:01:39,072
Postgres stays the truth, and Kafka only carries the news.

15
00:01:39,122 --> 00:01:43,272
Next, taking payments with no website at all: payment links.
